  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In a series <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LCR</mi></math> circuit, the voltage drop across the resistance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>, across the inductor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and across the capacitor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. Then,</p>\r"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "the voltage of the source will be leading the current in the circuit",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "the voltage drop across each element will be less than the applied voltage",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "the power factor of the circuit will be <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "none of these",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since, <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math><br /><br />(Also <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow></math> can never be greater than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>)<br /><br />Hence, (c) is wrong<br /><br />Also <math><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></math><br /><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi></math> Current will be leading.<br /><br />In an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LCR</mi></math> circuit,<br /><br /><math><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msqrt><msup><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></msqrt></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>, which is less than the voltage drop across the capacitor."
  }
